    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>“Rebirth” was the period’s own idea (Vasari’s <em>rinascita</em>, 1550); “the Renaissance” as an era is a 19th-century name, made famous by Burckhardt in 1860.</li>
        <li>Italy was ready because of its self-governing cities, its commercial wealth and its daily contact with Roman ruins and law.</li>
        <li>Petrarch gave the movement its central idea: antiquity as light, the following centuries as darkness, and his own time as a recovery.</li>
        <li>That idea was bold but unfair; the “Middle Ages” preserved the classics and had revivals of their own.</li>
        <li>The humanists hunted books, learned Greek, built schools and wrote for public life; artists and architects made the revival visible.</li>
        <li>Historians still debate how new the Renaissance was. This course takes the change as real, the roots as old, and imitation as a danger.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">This reopens Ring 3, the city, a thousand years after Rome, and opens a new ring of learning across borders. The next lesson meets the man who started it: Petrarch.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="failed-title">
      <h2 id="failed-title">What failed, and what the next age learned</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>An empire that could not hold.</strong> The fall of Rome in the West broke the great ring of the country and empire. Carried forward: civilization survived in smaller units, cities, monasteries and schools, which could rebuild when the time came.</li>
        <li><strong>Contempt for the recent past.</strong> The humanists dismissed the centuries that had saved their books. Carried forward: a sound education honors those who preserved what it loves, and judges each age on the evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Learning for the few.</strong> The revival was the work of a narrow, wealthy elite, and its schools could train flatterers as easily as statesmen. Carried forward: printing, from about 1455, would carry the books far beyond that elite, and the best teachers tried to form judgment, not just style.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="glossary-title">
      <h2 id="glossary-title">Glossary</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Renaissance:</strong> French for “rebirth”; the revival of classical learning and art, beginning in Italy in the 1300s.</li>
        <li><strong>Rinascita:</strong> Italian for “rebirth”; Vasari’s word for the revival of the arts.</li>
        <li><strong>Commune:</strong> a self-governing association of citizens in a medieval Italian city.</li>
        <li><strong>Florin:</strong> the gold coin of Florence, first struck in 1252.</li>
        <li><strong>Humanist:</strong> a scholar of the <em>studia humanitatis</em>; not, in this period, an opponent of religion.</li>
        <li><strong>Studia humanitatis:</strong> grammar, rhetoric, poetry, history and moral philosophy.</li>
        <li><strong>Humanitas:</strong> the cultivated qualities that make a person fully human.</li>
        <li><strong>Middle Ages:</strong> the “middle” period between antiquity and its revival; a name that assumes a judgment.</li>
        <li><strong>Manuscript:</strong> a book copied by hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Linear perspective:</strong> a mathematical method for showing depth on a flat surface.</li>
        <li><strong>Republic of letters:</strong> the community of scholars across Europe who wrote to each other, mostly in Latin.</li>
        <li><strong>Avignon papacy:</strong> the period 1309–1377 when the popes lived at Avignon rather than Rome.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

